# 9. SECURITY AND DATABASE PERMISSIONS

Security is mandatory.

Configure Supabase Row Level Security (RLS) and appropriate policies.

Requirements:

- Anonymous visitors may read only published public content.
- Anonymous visitors must not insert, update, or delete administrative content.
- Only authorized authenticated admins may create, edit, publish, hide, or delete content.
- Only authorized admins may upload, replace, or delete administrative assets.
- Storage policies must follow the same permission model.
- Do not expose the Supabase service-role key in frontend environment variables or browser bundles.
- Use only public client credentials in the frontend.
- Keep sensitive operations server-side when needed.
- Validate all submitted content.
- Enforce the package limit through trusted backend validation, not just a disabled frontend button.
- Prevent unauthorized users from bypassing the admin login by calling database APIs directly.
- Do not use a client-side role flag as the sole source of authorization.
- Do not include secrets in logs, error messages, or public configuration.
- Avoid exposing private administrative information through public queries.

If server-side functions are necessary, use supported Supabase Edge Functions or an equivalent secure server-side mechanism.

Provide clear setup instructions for applying database migrations and RLS policies.

Do not mark the application as production-ready until the authentication, policies, storage permissions, and public/admin access have been checked.

